<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch05_P03_Trichotomy_lines</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here is the proof from the example above that the square of every real number is non-negative, written in numbered lines. The three cases may come in a different order from the example.</p>
<div>
<p><strong>(1)</strong> {@pl[1]@}</p>
<p><strong>(2)</strong> {@pl[2]@}</p>
<p><strong>(3)</strong> {@pl[3]@}</p>
<p><strong>(4)</strong> {@pl[4]@}</p>
<p><strong>(5)</strong> {@pl[5]@}</p>
<p><strong>(6)</strong> {@pl[6]@}</p>
<p><strong>(7)</strong> {@pl[7]@}</p>
<p><strong>(8)</strong> {@pl[8]@}</p>
<p><strong>(9)</strong> {@pl[9]@}</p>
</div>
<ol type="a">
<li><p>In which line is the rule &#8216;multiplying both sides of an inequality by a negative number reverses the inequality&#8217; used?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>The last line concludes \(a^2 \ge 0\), not \(a^2 > 0\). Which line is the reason the conclusion needs \(\ge\)?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<ol type="a">
<li><p>In line {@lineN2@}, the case \(a \lt 0\), both sides of \(a \lt 0\) are multiplied by \(a\), which is negative in this case, so the inequality reverses: \(a \lt 0\) becomes \(a \times a > 0 \times a\). Line {@lineP2@} has the same algebra but keeps the direction, because there \(a\) is positive.</p></li>
<li><p>The conclusion needs \(\ge\) because of the case \(a = 0\) (lines {@lineZ1@} and {@lineZ2@}), where \(a^2 = 0\); the other two cases give \(a^2 > 0\). Together the three cases give \(a^2 \ge 0\) for every real number.</p></li>
</ol>]]></text></generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1000000</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026062900</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables><text><![CDATA[/* The three case blocks of the trichotomy proof in a random order: 1 = a > 0, 2 = a < 0, 3 = a = 0. */
corder: random_permutation([1, 2, 3]);
posP: sublist_indices(corder, lambda([b], b = 1))[1];
posN: sublist_indices(corder, lambda([b], b = 2))[1];
posZ: sublist_indices(corder, lambda([b], b = 3))[1];
lineP1: 1 + 2*posP; lineP2: lineP1 + 1;
lineN1: 1 + 2*posN; lineN2: lineN1 + 1;
lineZ1: 1 + 2*posZ; lineZ2: lineZ1 + 1;
blockP: ["Case \\(a > 0\\).", "Multiplying both sides of \\(a > 0\\) by \\(a\\) gives \\(a \\times a > 0 \\times a\\), so \\(a^2 > 0\\)."];
blockN: ["Case \\(a \\lt 0\\).", "Multiplying both sides of \\(a \\lt 0\\) by \\(a\\) gives \\(a \\times a > 0 \\times a\\), so \\(a^2 > 0\\)."];
blockZ: ["Case \\(a = 0\\).", "Then \\(a^2 = 0 \\times 0 = 0\\)."];
caseblocks: [blockP, blockN, blockZ];
pl: append(["Let \\(a\\) be any real number.", "By trichotomy, \\(a > 0\\), \\(a \\lt 0\\) or \\(a = 0\\)."],
  caseblocks[corder[1]], caseblocks[corder[2]], caseblocks[corder[3]],
  ["So in every case \\(a^2 > 0\\) or \\(a^2 = 0\\); that is, \\(a^2 \\ge 0\\) for every real number \\(a\\)."]);
validate_line(ex) := if integerp(ex) and ex >= 1 and ex <= 9 then "" else "Enter a line number from 1 to 9.";
s_assert(is(length(unique([lineN2, lineP2, lineN1, 2, 9])) = 5), true);
]]></text></questionvariables>
